## 2.1. Task Details

**Step 0 precedes this ticket's real subject.** It is not a linting step — it is
the audit result that Steps 1, 10, 14 and 20 silently depend on. Steps 10, 14 and
20.3 each propose a shared "displayed code" exemption keyed on `*.code.ts` /
`codeSnippets.ts`, and six of those files were named `codeSnippets.ts`, which is
not a role. Step 0.1 settles the name those three steps must then key on.

**Step 0.9–0.14 absorbed the `server/` and `common/` lint debt.** Widening the
script to `eslint src server common` surfaced **124 errors** that `eslint src` had
never reached. Only 23 of them belong to this ticket's own rules; the rest is
accumulated debt from trees that were unreachable, and it is now Step 0's work by
explicit decision. The families:

| Count | Family                                                                                                                                     | Grouped under |
| ----- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------- |
| 23    | `standards/*` — suffix, newline-in-imports, empty-row-in-jsx                                                                               | 0.10          |
| 9     | `no-unused-vars`, mostly `_`-prefixed                                                                                                      | 0.11          |
| 5     | unused `eslint-disable` directives                                                                                                         | 0.11          |
| ~19   | `no-unnecessary-type-assertion`                                                                                                            | 0.12          |
| ~45   | `no-unsafe-*` (`any` from Mongoose and `req`/socket)                                                                                       | 0.13          |
| 8     | `require-await`                                                                                                                            | 0.14          |
| ~15   | `no-floating-promises`, `no-misused-promises`, `only-throw-error`, `no-base-to-string`, `no-unused-expressions`, `no-unexpected-multiline` | 0.14          |

Two of these are **config gaps, not code defects**: the `_`-prefix convention and
positionally-required middleware args are reported because no ignore pattern is
set. Clearing them by editing code would destroy deliberate markers.

**`no-unsafe-*` and `no-unnecessary-type-assertion` can demand opposite things at
one site** — one wants a narrower type, the other says the cast you would add is
redundant. Where they collide the type is fixed, never the assertion re-added.
That is 0.12.3 and it is the rule that keeps the sweep from trading type debt for
lint-passing noise.

**Step 0 is complete — 124 errors to 0.** Widening the script to `eslint src server
common` exposed 124 accumulated errors. All are cleared, and `tsc` and lint are both
green. Three findings decided the sweep and are worth reading before Step 1:

- **`extends Document` without a type argument makes `_id` `any`.** `Document<T = any>`
  is generic over its id type, so omitting it makes every `_id` read unsafe — that single
  defect accounted for most of the `no-unsafe-*` group, including `Schedule`'s ten.
  `Document<mongoose.Types.ObjectId>` fixes it, and then exposes real `ObjectId`-vs-`string`
  bugs that `any` had been hiding. Twelve interfaces still carry the defect — see §3.
- **`tsc` clean does not mean lint clean, and the editor lies both ways.** The type-aware
  pass resolves a few symbols differently from the compiler. Every probe run through the
  compiler settled a question; every guess made by reading did not.
- **Two `standards/*` violations were config gaps, not code defects** — the `_`-prefix
  convention and unused `eslint-disable` directives. They were closed by config, not by
  editing the code they flagged.

**Step 16 and Step 17 both touch `Contact.schema.ts`.** Landing 17 first is the
cheaper order: it is the smaller sweep, and it rewrites the `.min(10, 'Min 10
characters')` call that Step 16.12 would otherwise fix twice.

**Step 20 has 50 violations across 24 files**, but roughly half are displayed
