## 3. Decisions & Rationale

- **A bare filename is legal when its folder already states the context.**
  `file-structure.md` §1.1 documents `components/ComponentFoo.tsx` — a component is named for
  the component, not a role or its folder — so the rule allows a bare name inside a grouping
  folder (`components`, `utils`, `tests`). That list is extracted to `rules/constants.ts` and
  read by both this rule and `no-misnamed-primary-file`, which had the same list inline.
  Without it the tighten produced 187 false positives, almost all component files.
- **`src/` was not role-conforming, and `no-ad-hoc-file-suffix` could not tell.** Its
  extension-only pattern accepted any bare name, so `references.ts` passed while stating no
  role. That is why 0.1–0.3 could not be verified from a green lint, and why the bare-name
  ban is the change that makes "role file" a decidable question.
- **The `Word/` folder collapses.** `wordGroups.ts`, `getAnagramKey.ts` and
  `getPossibleWords.ts` are all the `utils` role, so §1.2's `Feature.utils.ts` expresses them
  as one file exporting a single grouped value. `constants.ts` has five bare consts and
  becomes `Word.constants.ts`. Consolidating now satisfies Step 1 rather than renaming and
  rediscovering it: a `utils` file exporting two values is exactly what `exactly-one-export`
  flags, and `getPossibleWords.ts` exporting `transformAnagramMap` is a name the file does
  not define. `Types/Level.ts` and `Types/Words.ts` fold the same way into `Word.types.ts`.
- **`LocalSession.ts` is the session's slice of `localStorage`, so it stays with Session.**
  It shares `APP_KEY` with `App.provider.tsx` and mirrors `WordDuelArena`'s device-id slice,
  so the folder is right and only the role was missing — `Session.repository.ts`, §1.2's
  "data access". Moving it to `common/` would put session knowledge in shared code. The three
  slices reimplementing "read my key, update my slice" is a `/generalise` candidate.
- **`src/router.tsx` is a routes file, not an entry point** — it exports `routes` and
  `router`, and §1.2's `Feature.routes.tsx` is documented as a keyed map plus a derived
  array, so two exports is the shape. Renamed `src/router.routes.tsx`.
- **`extends Document` must carry its id type.** Twelve `*.types.ts` interfaces still
  declare `extends Document` with no argument, which makes `_id` `any` and trips five
  `no-unsafe-*` rules at every `_id` read. `server/App/Users/Users.types.ts` is the one
  fixed so far, and it took `server/App/Users` from 10 errors to 0. `.github/skills/db/`
  carries the defect too, so the `db` skill teaches it and should be corrected with them.
  The remainder are latent rather than live — they only report where a caller reads `_id`.
- **`no-ad-hoc-file-suffix` gained `service`, `middlewares` and `**/\*.d.ts`as exclusions or
roles** rather than renaming files.`file-structure.md`§2.1 documents`Feature.middlewares.ts`as plural, so the rule was out of step with the doc. A declaration
file's name describes what it augments, not a role, so`.d.ts` leaves scope.
- **`no-unused-vars` needed an ignore pattern, not nine edits.** `@typescript-eslint`'s
  replacement for the core rule honours no `_` convention by default, unlike the ESLint
  original — which is why the project's own deliberate-unused marker was reported.
- **`no-base-to-string` fires on an `object` narrowing.** Where a guard has narrowed to
  bare `object`, `object` carries `Object.prototype.toString`; naming a
  `{ toString(): string }` binding, or narrowing straight from `unknown`, satisfies it.
  `Predicate.isObjectId` and `ApiTransformers.toApiValue` both needed this.
- **`Repository`'s `ModelLike` is generic over the document type.** Threading `D` through
  `makeCrud` removed four `as Promise<…>` casts that the type-aware pass saw through.
  No mongoose coupling was added, so `0004`'s duck-typed design stands.
- Step 0 absorbs the `server/` and `common/` lint debt rather than the debt
  becoming a separate ticket. Measured at 124 errors; only 23 are this ticket's
  own rules. The remainder is fixed properly — the `no-unsafe-*` boundary types
  are resolved rather than cast quiet, because a cast that satisfies the linter
  while leaving `any` in place contradicts `no-unnecessary-type-assertion` in the
  same run.
- The lint script widened from `eslint src` to `eslint src server common`, and
  `server/**` left the top-level `ignores`. Both were required: a CLI path filter
  cannot be widened by config, and a config `files` glob cannot reach a file the
  CLI never selected. The ignore alone changed nothing — the script was the gate
  that mattered.
- `no-ad-hoc-file-suffix` is the right home for the bare-source ban. The rule
  already owns "is this filename legal?" and a bare `.ts` is currently legal by
  omission, not by decision — the extension-only pattern accepts any name.
  Widening its scope to `server/` is what made 23 accumulated violations visible
  for the first time.
- `rules/readme.md` now documents the shipped `RuleRegistrar` shape — folder,
  entry, field table — after months of describing a `title` / `rationale` /
  `enforcement` shape that `types.ts` never implemented. The readme's folder
  shape states `tests/` as plural, matching the rename in
  `no-ad-hoc-file-suffix`.
- Step 1's id is `exactly-one-export`. The rule is a **filename lookup**, not a
  shape analysis, so the id describes the assertion rather than the file's role.
  The assertion is "exactly one", and a file exporting nothing fails it, so a
  `no-` id would state only half of what the rule enforces.
- Step 0 is a prerequisite, not a separate ticket, because Steps 10, 14 and 20.3
  each need to name the displayed-code files and would otherwise encode a name
  Step 0 is about to change.
- Step 2 overlaps `no-misnamed-primary-file`, which only covers the primary
  file. This rule generalises it to every role file, so the two must agree on
  naming or `no-misnamed-primary-file` should fold in.
- Step 4 covers §5 rows 8, 52 and 64 as one rule. `ui` and `db` each state the
  barrel convention for their own folder and `AGENTS.md` §1.4 states it for all
  of them — three sources, one assertion, so one rule and one spec.
- Each step verifies itself: sync, sweep, then a green `tsc` / lint / test run.
  A shared verification step at the end would report four rules' violations at
  once, and a failure would not name the rule that caused it.
- Step 7 gates Steps 8 to 10. `eslint.config.js` parses `**/*.{ts,tsx}` only, and
  `builder.ts` emits globs without inspecting them — so a rule scoped to `*.css`
  registers cleanly and then never fires. The breakpoints and the `#191b21` both
  live in `.css`, so the mechanism has to be chosen before those steps are built.
- Step 11 is `warn` rather than `error`. `Const.Font` is `em`-based, so a `px`
  size may be legitimate where Steps 8 and 9 have live violations to prove the
  rule bites. It rises to `error` once the sweep comes back clean.
- Step 13 is a **placement** rule, not a prohibition. `test` already gives each
  kind of setup a home — `.mocks.ts` for data, `.mockHandlers.ts` for handlers,
  `.spec.utils.ts` for labels, render helpers and interaction helpers. The rule
  asks whether a helper is in the right file, not whether the region above
  `describe` is empty. `Login.spec.tsx` shows why the distinction matters: its
  imports are already correct, and only `setupLogin` is misplaced.
- Step 13.3 allows `vi.mock` and `vi.mocked` because they **cannot** hoist.
  `vi.mock` is hoisted above the imports by the transform, so moving it to
  another module mocks that module's imports instead. `vi.mocked` depends on the
  call above it. The exception is a constraint of the mocking model, not a
  preference.
- Step 14 starts as `warn`, not `error`. The skill draws its example from a call
  body — `waitFor(() => expect(…))` — but the same shape appears in ordinary
  callbacks, where a brace is a reasonable choice and not a defect. Steps 14.2 to
  14.4 narrow it to the case the skill names, and 14.10 raises it to `error`
  once the sweep shows the narrowed form leaves nothing arguable behind.
- **A candidate must assert a value against a closed set, or a shape of the
  code itself.** It must not assert that one piece of code calls another. The
  first kind survives a refactor — change how `Const` is _used_ and the rule still
  holds, because the set is the invariant. The second kind encodes today's
  implementation and turns a lint failure into an architecture freeze.
- Step 15.6 exists because the key and the value are different things.
  `Gym.queries.ts` writes `Routines: { useGet: useGetGymUserRoutines }` — a
  conventional key over a feature-named function — and `Like.queries.ts` writes
  `usePost: usePostLike`. Checking the value would flag both, and both are right.
- Step 16.5 exempts `.min(1)`. `1` asserts presence — at least one item — rather
  than expressing a field's bound, so it has no constant to come from.
- Step 16 is the most heavily violated rule in this ticket: 14 hits across 5
  files. `Contact.schema.ts` shows why it earns its place — it declares
  `MAX_MESSAGE_CHARACTERS` and uses it on `message`, then writes `.max(50)` on
  `name` three lines above. The file demonstrates both forms side by side.
- The defect Step 16 prevents is drift, not tidiness. `.min(10, 'Min 10 characters')` writes
  the bound twice, so editing one copy makes the message lie. A constant lets the rule and
  its message read one value, which is why the `form` example composes the message from it.
- Step 17 passes the criterion above and a rejected candidate did not, which is
  why the test is worth stating. Both concern a message catalogue:
  `errorMessage(error, ClientMessage.Failure.Create('x'))` was **dropped**, since
  it asserts that one function's second argument calls another function — a call
  target, which a refactor invalidates. Step 17 **asserts a value against a
  closed set of six methods**, and a seventh method added later still satisfies
  it. Same subject, opposite verdicts.
- Step 17.10 is not a substitution step. `Contact.schema.ts` validates a phone
  format and a character set that no `ValidationMessage` method names, so the
  catalogue has to gain entries before those call sites can conform — the
  skill's own instruction, "if the catalogue lacks the verb, add it there".
- Step 18 has no conforming file except the skill's own example. Six components
  call `yupResolver` themselves — `Login`, `Register`, `Contact`, `Xmas2025`,
  `Categories` — and no real `*.schema.ts` exports a resolver. The rule is stated
  in `form` and demonstrated in `form/Feature.schema.ts`, and the codebase has
  never adopted it, so the sweep is a genuine six-file change rather than a
  tidy-up.
- Step 18.4 exists because a schema may be a factory. `Categories.schema.ts`
  takes `parentOptions` and builds the schema per call, so a single exported
  resolver cannot exist — the factory's shape has to be mirrored by a resolver
  factory. The rule must accept that rather than demand a constant.
- Step 19.4 narrows §1.2's wording, which reads absolutely and contradicts the
  line below it. §1.2 also requires `import type` in the type-only group, so a
  file with both a value and a type from one path _must_ write two imports —
  `queries/Feature.transformers.ts` does exactly that. The rule therefore counts
  per path **and** kind, flagging a redundant split while leaving the mandated
  one alone. ESLint's built-in `no-duplicate-imports` is not enabled today, and
  its default reading would flag that file, so the rule is written rather than
  switched on.
- Step 20.3 exists because three rules now need the same exemption. Steps 10, 14
  and 20 each flag something a displayed code sample legitimately contains —
  `zIndex: 1000`, a braced callback, a `for` loop. The `*.code.ts` /
  `codeSnippets.ts` convention already marks those files, so the exemption is one
  shared list rather than three.
- Step 20 is the first rule in this ticket whose sweep may not finish cleanly.
  §1.3 says "Do not: `for`, `while`" without an exception, but the shuffle and the
  digit parse are arithmetic over an index, which `map` / `filter` / `reduce`
  express poorly. Steps 20.4 and 20.10 exist so each site is judged rather than
  converted by pattern, and so a suppression carries a reason.
- Step 12 lands on **302** existing violations across 23 spec files — the
  largest sweep in this ticket by an order of magnitude. The rule is right and
  the naming is consistent once applied, but Step 12.9 is a real decision:
  rewriting 302 test names is a material change to files this ticket otherwise
  does not touch, and a name is a claim about behaviour that only a reader can
  verify. A suppression baseline is the alternative.
- Spacing stays out. `Const.Spacing` is thirteen steps, and a `600px` layout
  width or a `1px` hairline is not a spacing step — `ui`'s own example writes
  both. Breakpoints, colours, z-index and font size are closed sets, so a value
  outside them is always wrong; spacing is not, so it stays with `review-file`.
- **`no-unused-vars` took the API down**, and it is this ticket's doing. Enabling
  the rule at `eslint.config.js:46-48` left `args` at its default `'after-used'`,
  which reports only arguments **after the last used one**. In
  `AppMiddleware.error` — `(err, req, res, next)`, where the body reads only `err`
  and `res` — that flagged exactly one name: `next`. Deleting it to go green
  turned an Express error handler into ordinary middleware, because Express
  identifies one by `fn.length === 4`; the handler then received `(req, res, next)`,
  so `res` was `next` and every request died on `res.status is not a function`.
  Restored as `_next: NextFunction` — the prefix the same rule's
  `argsIgnorePattern` exists for. Blast radius was one file: `grep '\(err'`
  across `server/**` returns this handler and two catch blocks. A parameter can be
  load-bearing by **position**, not by use, so an unused-argument sweep must
  prefix rather than delete.
- **`role-export-name` is driven by one map, not by derivation.** `{Stem}` is the
  file's first dot-segment and each role carries one strict template, so
  `Word.repository.ts` reads `WordRepository`. Strict was chosen over per-file
  exceptions: `Xmas.constants.ts` exporting `XmasFieldLimits` gets renamed rather than
  excused. A composite key wins over a single one, so `Form.spec.utils.tsx`
  reads `{Stem}TestUtils`, never `{Stem}Utils`.
- **A name that carries no role asserts nothing.** A spec names nothing, an unmapped
  role (`types`) names nothing, and a bare name that does not start uppercase names
  nothing — which keeps `index.ts`, `main.tsx` and `setupTests.ts` out with no list
  to maintain. A bare PascalCase name is a feature file and must export itself:
  `Blog.tsx` exports `Blog`.
- **That PascalCase guard hid three files, and the fold retired two of them.**
  `tests/useTableController.mocks.ts` and `tests/useTableController.spec.utils.tsx` are
  gone: the controller folded into `common/ux/Table/Table.hooks.ts`, so its specs are the
  role-named `Table.hooks.*` and `role-export-name` reaches them again. `src/router.routes.tsx`
  no longer exists either, so the offender list needs re-measuring when the rule is built.
  Every other lowercase stem is skipped by another branch already — `.d.ts`, a spec, or the
  unmapped `types`. Step 22 closes the gap by turning the convention into a rule
  instead of an escape.
- **The rules carry their own `predicates.ts` and `generics.ts`.** A rule cannot
  reach `common/utils` at runtime: the sources sit in `rules/` while the emitted
  plugin runs from `rules/dist/`, one level deeper, so a relative specifier that
  compiles resolves one directory too high. The two files mirror the checks the
  rules make — `isDefined`, `isEmpty`, `isNonEmpty`, `isString`; `Nil`, `Defined`,
  `Optional`, `Nullable`. A rule folder keeps `constants` / `types` / `utils`
  separate so `rules.ts` holds the rule and nothing else.
- **A package and an alias are told apart by a list, not by the `@`.**
  `@tanstack/react-query` carries `@` exactly as `@common-utils` does, so the rule carries
  `AliasPrefixes` and the three files that declare aliases each warn that a new one belongs
  in it: `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `jest.config.cjs`. Reading `paths` at lint time
  was dropped — the drift is loud (a missing alias makes that path rank as a package, and
  every file using it reports), and the warning sits where the alias is added.
- **Assets stay a suffix list.** A deny-list of module extensions cannot carry this:
  `./Settings.types` is a module whose name reads as an extension. The list is broad —
  styles, images, sound, video, fonts, documents, models, shaders — so a new file type is
  one line rather than a redesign, and the standard points at the list instead of repeating it.
- **A bare `'.'` is same-directory and a bare `'..'` is parent-relative.** Both were falling
  through to the external rank because the checks matched only `'./'` and `'../'`. They are
  relative specifiers, so they rank with the rest of the relative group.
- **An all-inline-type statement is not a mixed kind.** `import { type Option } from 'x'`
  carries one kind, so `no-mixed-import-kind` leaves it alone and its spec pins that. Only a
  statement holding a value and a type together is flagged.
- **The duplicate rule splits by statement family, not by counting both in one.**
  `no-duplicate-import` covers imports and `no-duplicate-export` covers re-exports, so each id
  names exactly what it checks and neither has to explain the other's bucket.
- **A type-only statement is counted by its shape.** TS1363 forbids one type-only import from
  carrying a default and named bindings together, so `import type mongoose from 'mongoose'`
  beside `import type { Document } from 'mongoose'` is the only way to name both — the two
  shapes are separate groups inside the `type` kind. Value imports stay strict: `mongoose`
  beside `{ Document }` merges into one statement, so it still reports.
- **A hand sweep must not delete a statement it cannot merge.** Folding one path's imports
  removed the `mongoose` `Document` import, which turned `extends Document` into the DOM type,
  and `toApiResource` then rejected every routine; the same pass dropped `ApiResponder` from
  `Routines.service.ts` while the body still called it. Both are restored — and `server/**`
  sits outside `npm run tsc`, so only the editor saw either.
- **A re-export ranks below a local declaration.** `export { BrowserHooks } from './Browser.hooks'`
  is a named re-export (style 5) while `export const Browser = {…}` is a named declaration
  (style 6), so a folder barrel that builds its value from imports puts the re-exports first and
  `export type` last. The same edge caught `Form/index.ts` and `common/utils/Browser`.
- **The commit gate lints what changed and compiles what exists.** ESLint reads one file at a
  time, so running it over the staged files costs what the commit touches and still catches
  every rule the registry ships. `tsc` and the tests cannot be narrowed the same way: a type
  error surfaces in the file that _uses_ a changed signature, and a spec breaks in a file the
  commit never names — so both run in full, and the gate stays honest at the price of speed.
- **No SCSS in `common/` was dropped as a rule.** It is an engineering decision — shared UI
  depends on no preprocessor — not a code convention the linter should own, and
  `.github/skills/ui/SKILL.md` already states it twice: "Common UI uses CSS. Do not introduce
  SCSS into `@common`", and SCSS itself is retiring. `common/` holds zero `.scss` today, so the
  rule would guard a boundary nobody has crossed while the sentence that owns the decision sits
  where the decision is made.
- **A processor answers Step 7 without a second engine.** ESLint only opens `.ts` and `.tsx`, and
  a non-ESLint checker would mean a second linter kind in the registry. A processor reads a
  stylesheet and emits one JavaScript line per stylesheet line — `property: value` becomes
  `({ "<property>": "<value>" })`, everything else `void 0;` — so a rule sees the same
  `Property` node on both surfaces and line numbers still point at the stylesheet. CSS and SCSS
  are now ordinary lint targets, and Steps 8, 9 and 11 have nowhere new to go.
- **The processor names no property.** Its first draft hard-coded `z-index`, which would have
  meant editing the processor for every rule that followed. Generalising it to any declaration
  moved that knowledge back into the rule, where it belongs.
- **z-index is checked by provenance, not by value.** A literal is refused even when it equals a
  layer, because a number that happens to match today breaks silently the moment the layers
  move. The stylesheet half demands `var(--z-*)` and the tokens now live in `common/ux/index.css`
  beside the palette, which also repaired `Screen.css`'s `var(--z-sticky)`, undefined until then.
- **Alphabetical barrels were dropped.** Step 25 already fixes _types last_ and the path
  order; what it deliberately leaves free is the order inside one style and one path. Adding
  an alphabetical tie-break means every new export of a barrel potentially moves the line
  above it, so a barrel that passes today fails the moment a symbol is added — the rule would
  cost work on every addition and buy nothing the style-then-path order does not already give.
  `AGENTS.md` §1.4 said "alphabetical" and now says what the rule actually enforces.
- **A barrel cannot carry one name as both a value and a type from two files.**
  `export { Option } from './Option'` beside `export type { Option } from './Option.types'`
  hands consumers only the value, so `Option<T>` degraded to `error` and every `onSelect`
  callback downstream lost its parameter type. A type and its value must share one module —
  the value first, the type below it — and the barrel re-export that single file.
- **`'../X'` from a `tests/` folder means the file, not the folder.** TypeScript prefers
  `Icon.tsx` over `Icon/index.ts`, so a spec naming `'../Icon'` reads the component and misses
  the types its barrel carries. Specs import `'..'` for the index, or `'../X.types'` for the
  type when the barrel re-exports it from a second file.
- **A barrel's export list is the contract its specs rely on.** `Predicate`'s index re-exports
  23 of the predicates its own spec uses, so moving that spec to `'..'` broke 100 call sites.
  Sweeping import paths must first check that the target barrel actually carries every symbol.
- **`no-qualified-type` reports a namespace that was never imported, not every namespace.**
  The first draft flagged any `Namespace.Type`, which made `JSX.Element` and
  `keyof JSX.IntrinsicElements` unfixable: React exports the `JSX` namespace, not its members,
  so there is no `Element` to import. The rule now collects the file's import bindings and
  reports only when the first name in the chain is absent from them. Every site the sweep found
  was a UMD-global reach-through — `React.`, `Joi.`, `yup.`, `globalThis.` — with no import
  present, so all of them still report, while a qualified type whose namespace is imported
  passes. The heritage half reads `MemberExpression` because `TSHeritageBase.expression` is an
  `Expression`, not a `TSQualifiedName`.
- **Step 3 is deferred, not dropped.** It asks whether a role file has its own spec, and the
  answer depends on which role files exist — which Steps 12 to 22 are still deciding. Landing it
  now fixes a spec baseline against a tree that is about to move, then re-measures after every
  later step. It resumes once the other rules have landed, feature by feature, and re-reads
  `file-structure.md` §1.3 and §2.5 first: both tables changed while the step waited.
- **`test(…)` is banned, not tolerated as an alias.** Vitest treats `it` and `test` as one
  function, so the rule recognises both and reports the second: a ban that only ignored the
  alias would let `test('should render')` pass while the skill says `it`. The skill now states
  it too — `test` is the same function under a name this codebase does not use, and the three
  call sites it had were in `Welcome.spec.tsx`.
- **A subject-led name bends into `should have <subject> <verb>`, and that reads badly.** The
  rule wants `should <verb>` first, so `ApiResponder error methods > should have badRequest
produce a 400 ApiError` was the first attempt — a workaround, not English. `describe` carries
  the subject instead, and `ApiResponder.spec.ts` now nests by method:
  `ApiResponder error methods > badRequest > should produce a 400 ApiError from a string`.
  Where the assertion already repeats the subject — `should call axios.get …`, `should delegate
to FakeModel.find …` — the leading method word was redundant and simply came out, so
  `RequestBuilder` and `Repository` needed no nesting.
- **A 908-name migration is a tool job with a human tail.** A throwaway codemod read the lint
  report, located the name literal at each reported call and rewrote the mechanical verbs —
  `returns` → `should return`, `does not X` → `should not X`, `is X` → `should be X` — which
  covered **746** of the **908**. The remaining **162** were names that had never been
  sentences, and those were written by hand. Nothing was suppressed, so the rule's first day in
  `main` is the same run that proves it.

## 4. Open Questions

- Step 1: does "only one value" mean one `export` statement, or one exported
  binding where a type-only export alongside a value is allowed?
  Status: Answered — the rule is driven by a list of filenames, not by the
  language semantics of the export statement. A filename in the list must
  export exactly one thing; `routes.tsx` is an entry decision, not an
  exception carved into the rule.
- Step 3: is a missing spec an `error` or a `warn` while the direct-spec gap is
  still being closed?
  Status: Answered — the step is deferred (§3), so the severity is settled when it resumes.
- Step 4: where does an `export *` line sort? It has no symbol to sort by.
  `common/ux/index.ts` mixes both forms.
  Status: Answered by Step 25 — a plain `export *` is a star re-export (style 2) and
  `export type *` carries `exportKind: 'type'`, so it ranks type-only and sorts last. Step 4
  narrows to the alphabetical tie-break inside one style and path.
- `file-structure.md` §1.2 says a component's props type sits above the component and is
  "never moved to `.types.ts`". Step 25 requires exported types last, and the `common/ux` and
  `shared/components` sweep put those props in `Component.types.ts` beside it.
  Status: Open — the doc line needs correcting before another component copies the old shape.
- Step 0: is the convention filename-based or folder-based? The `WordDuelArena`
  server tree carries its role in the folder, and the `common/` and `rules/`
  areas are 100% filename-conforming. Step 0.6 decides.
  Status: Open
- Step 0: do `service`, `middlewares` and `models` join the allowed role list, or
  do the files rename to `middleware` and `model`? The list currently holds the
  singular forms only.
  Status: Open

